        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Run Time Data Capture Logic"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1764840091965"
          },
          "code": "RUN_TIME_DATA_CAPTURE_LOGIC",
          "id": "1764840129533",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1764840129534_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "---------------> Input <---------------\nObject Data, Meeting Notes, General Notes, Emails, Marketing and Sales Activities can be found in:\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.context}}\n\nPreviously derived attributes and their values can be found in:\n{{$context.$nodes.FETCH_ENGAGEMENT_INSIGHT_ROW.$output.additionalInsightData}}\n\nData Capture Attributes and their aiInstructions / evaluationText (including any possible value lists) can be found in:\n{{$context.$nodes.FETCH_DATA_CAPTURE_RULE.$output}}\n\n\n--------------> COMMUNICATION_HISTORY (Definition) <-------------------\n1. Combine all content from Object Data, Meeting Notes, General Notes, Emails, Marketing and Sales Activities into a single dataset called COMMUNICATION_HISTORY.\n2. Remove non-semantic elements such as:\n\t- Email signatures\n\t- Disclaimers\n\t- Forwarded/quoted reply blocks\n\t- Greetings, closings, and boilerplate text\n3. Deduplicate identical or near-identical lines.\n4. COMMUNICATION_HISTORY is the only source allowed for deriving attribute values.\n5. If COMMUNICATION_HISTORY has no explicit information for an attribute → apply the Default Value Rules.\n\n-------------> Recency Rules <------------------\nDetermine “most recent” in this order:\n1. Explicit timestamps or dates inside the text\n2. Document-level timestamps (e.g., email date > meeting notes > general notes)\n3. If timestamps are unavailable, later appearance in COMMUNICATION_HISTORY is more recent\n4. If recency cannot be determined when statements conflict, apply the Default Value Rules\n\n-------------> Explicitness Rules <------------------\nA statement is considered explicit only if:\n\t- It directly states the value or\n\t- It clearly matches synonyms, cues, or patterns listed in the attribute’s aiInstructions / evaluationText.\nIf any interpretation, assumption, or guess would be required → treat as not explicit → apply the Default Value Rules.\n\n-------------> Your Task <------------------\nFor each data capture attribute:\n1. Strictly analyze COMMUNICATION_HISTORY using the attribute’s aiInstructions / evaluationText.\n2. Follow only explicit statements; do NOT infer, interpret, or guess anything.\n3. Evaluate each attribute independently of all others.\n4. If multiple explicit statements match → choose the most recent using the Recency Rules.\n5. If conflicting statements exist with no determinable recency → apply the Default Value Rules.\n\n-------------> Default Value Rules <------------------\nFor every attribute:\n- If explicit evidence exists in COMMUNICATION_HISTORY, return the matched value.\n- If no explicit evidence matches, use the previously derived value.\n- If no previously derived value exists, check the aiInstructions / evaluationText for a default value.\n- If a default value is explicitly present there, return that default value exactly.\n- If no default value is explicitly present there, return null.\n\n---------> Attribute Output Rules <------------\nEach attribute's aiInstructions / evaluationText may contain either:\n(A) Defined possible values embedded in the text\n(e.g., “Choose one of: YES, NO, UNKNOWN…”)\nIf so:\n\t- Extract the allowed values from the aiInstructions or evaluationText text\n\t- Choose exactly one allowed value based on explicit evidence\n\t- If no explicit evidence matches → apply the Default Value Rules.\n\t- Do NOT output any value not listed in aiInstructions or evaluationText\n(B) No possible values included in aiInstructions or evaluationText\n(meaning: no defined value list exists)\nIn that case:\n\t- Treat the attribute as free-form\n\t- Output a reasoning summary (≤100 characters) based strictly on explicit statements\n\t- If no explicit evidence exists → apply the Default Value Rules.\n\n-------------------> Output Format (strict JSON only) <--------------------\nOutput only this JSON and nothing else (no commentary before or after):  \n{\n  \"dataCapture\": {\n    \"opportunities\": {\n      \"<fieldName1>\": \"<fieldValue1>\",\n      \"<fieldName2>\": \"<fieldValue2>\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n\n---------------> Example (only for reference) <-----------------\n{\n  \"dataCapture\": {\n    \"opportunities\": {\n      \"BudgetStatus_c\": \"Y\",\n      \"CustomerCommit_c\": \"IN_QUARTER\",\n      \"WhyBuyNow_c\": null,\n      \"BudgetAmount\": null,\n      \"WinProb\": 54,\n      \"EffectiveDate\": null,\n      \"LastUpdateDate\": \"2026-04-05\",\n      \"WhyBuyAnything_c\": \"Their ERP is failing and causing delays and hence planning to buy Fusion ERP\"\n    }\n  }\n}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Return Output"
          },
          "outcomes": {},
          "code": "RETURN_OUTPUT",
          "id": "1764840091965",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1764840091968_1",
              "name": "returnValue",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{($context.$triggers.REST.$input.engagementNumber != '' && $context.$nodes.FETCH_DATA_CAPTURE_RULE.$output.items.length) ? $context.$nodes.RUN_TIME_DATA_CAPTURE_LOGIC.$output : null}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "RETURN"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Run in Parallel"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "0": "1764840897147",
            "1": "1764840924698",
            "2": "add_1764840472937_2"
          },
          "code": "PARALLEL_1764840472938",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1764840129533",
          "id": "1764840472937",
          "inputs": [],
